window.FAST_BANK = window.FAST_BANK || {};
FAST_BANK.vocab = [
  {"id":"voc-001","skill":"v.context","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"Maya was so **reluctant** to jump into the cold spring that she stood on the dock for ten minutes.\"\n\nWhat does **reluctant** mean?","o":["excited to try something new","too tired to move","proud of what you did","not wanting to do something"],"a":3,"e":"Maya waited a long time on the dock instead of jumping. That clue shows she did not want to do it, so **reluctant** means \"not wanting to do something.\""},
  {"id":"voc-002","skill":"v.context","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"The dance studio was **spacious**, with plenty of room for all twenty dancers to spin without bumping into each other.\"\n\nWhat does **spacious** mean?","o":["brightly painted","having lots of room","very noisy","crowded and very tight"],"a":1,"e":"The clue \"plenty of room for all twenty dancers\" tells you **spacious** means having lots of room."},
  {"id":"voc-003","skill":"v.context","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"After the long hike, Leo was **famished**, so he ate two sandwiches, an apple, and a big bowl of rice.\"\n\nWhat does **famished** mean?","o":["very hungry","very sleepy","a little bored","feeling sick"],"a":0,"e":"Leo ate a LOT of food. That clue shows he was **famished**, or very hungry."},
  {"id":"voc-004","skill":"v.context","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"The squishy panda toy was so **pliable** that Ava could bend it, twist it, and squeeze it into a tiny ball.\"\n\nWhat does **pliable** mean?","o":["hard and stiff like a rock","soft, furry, and fuzzy","easy to bend and shape","small and round"],"a":2,"e":"Ava could bend, twist, and squeeze the toy. Those actions are clues that **pliable** means easy to bend and shape."},
  {"id":"voc-005","skill":"v.context","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"The coach's instructions were so **vague** that nobody knew where to stand or when to start running.\"\n\nWhat does **vague** mean?","o":["carefully planned","very loud","too long","not clear"],"a":3,"e":"Nobody knew what to do, so the instructions must not have been clear. That is what **vague** means."},
  {"id":"voc-006","skill":"v.context","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"The manatee moved at a **leisurely** pace, drifting slowly through the warm water and stopping often to munch on sea grass.\"\n\nWhat does **leisurely** mean?","o":["relaxed and unhurried","lost and confused","strong and very powerful","quick and nervous"],"a":0,"e":"The words \"drifting slowly\" and \"stopping often\" show the manatee was in no hurry. **Leisurely** means relaxed and unhurried."},
  {"id":"voc-007","skill":"v.context","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"Grandma's kitchen was always **immaculate**; not a single crumb sat on the counter, and the pots sparkled.\"\n\nWhat does **immaculate** mean?","o":["small and cozy","full of good smells","perfectly clean","very old"],"a":2,"e":"No crumbs and sparkling pots are clues. **Immaculate** means perfectly clean."},
  {"id":"voc-008","skill":"v.context","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"The team was **elated** when they won the soccer championship, cheering and hugging on the field.\"\n\nWhat does **elated** mean?","o":["a little worried","very happy","tired out","very surprised"],"a":1,"e":"Cheering and hugging are things people do when they are thrilled. **Elated** means very happy."},
  {"id":"voc-009","skill":"v.context","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"Because the trail was **treacherous**, the ranger warned hikers to watch for loose rocks and slippery mud.\"\n\nWhat does **treacherous** mean?","o":["beautiful","crowded","very short","dangerous"],"a":3,"e":"Loose rocks and slippery mud could make someone fall. Those clues show **treacherous** means dangerous."},
  {"id":"voc-010","skill":"v.context","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"Priya tried to **conceal** her birthday gift for her brother by hiding it under a pile of towels in the closet.\"\n\nWhat does **conceal** mean?","o":["hide","buy","wrap","show"],"a":0,"e":"The word \"hiding\" is a great clue right in the sentence. **Conceal** means to hide."},
  {"id":"voc-011","skill":"v.context","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"The kitten was **timid** at first, hiding behind the couch whenever a visitor came into the room.\"\n\nWhat does **timid** mean?","o":["playful and very silly","shy and easily scared","sleepy and slow","hungry and grumpy"],"a":1,"e":"The kitten hid whenever someone came in. That clue tells you **timid** means shy and easily scared."},
  {"id":"voc-012","skill":"v.context","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"The baker had to **discard** the burnt cookies and throw them in the trash.\"\n\nWhat does **discard** mean?","o":["save for later","share with friends","get rid of","decorate"],"a":2,"e":"\"Throw them in the trash\" explains the word. To **discard** something means to get rid of it."},
  {"id":"voc-013","skill":"v.context","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"Our class plant began to **flourish** once we moved it to the sunny window; it grew three new leaves in a week.\"\n\nWhat does **flourish** mean?","o":["grow well","fall over","dry out","change color"],"a":0,"e":"Three new leaves in one week means the plant was growing strong. **Flourish** means to grow well."},
  {"id":"voc-014","skill":"v.context","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"The dancers' movements were perfectly **synchronized**, with every arm rising and falling at exactly the same time.\"\n\nWhat does **synchronized** mean?","o":["happening one after another","moving very slowly","happening at the same time","done without music"],"a":2,"e":"The clue \"at exactly the same time\" tells you what **synchronized** means."},
  {"id":"voc-015","skill":"v.context","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"Jamal was **astonished** when he opened the box and found a tiny turtle inside instead of a toy car.\"\n\nWhat does **astonished** mean?","o":["a bit disappointed","very bored","quite scared","very surprised"],"a":3,"e":"Finding a turtle when you expect a toy car would be a big surprise! **Astonished** means very surprised."},
  {"id":"voc-016","skill":"v.context","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"The heavy rain caused the river to **overflow**, and water spread across the park and onto the sidewalks.\"\n\nWhat does **overflow** mean?","o":["turn muddy","spill over the edges","dry up in the hot sun","freeze solid"],"a":1,"e":"Water spread onto the park and sidewalks, so it must have spilled over the river's edges."},
  {"id":"voc-017","skill":"v.context","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"The old bridge was too **fragile** to hold a car, so only people on foot were allowed to cross.\"\n\nWhat does **fragile** mean?","o":["easily broken","strong and safe","freshly painted","very long"],"a":0,"e":"Cars were not allowed because the bridge might break. **Fragile** means easily broken."},
  {"id":"voc-018","skill":"v.context","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"Mr. Ortiz gave a **brief** speech that lasted less than a minute, and then the science fair began.\"\n\nWhat does **brief** mean?","o":["important","short","boring","funny"],"a":1,"e":"\"Less than a minute\" is a clue that the speech was short. **Brief** means short."},
  {"id":"voc-019","skill":"v.context","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"The gator lay **motionless** on the riverbank, so still that some visitors thought it was a statue.\"\n\nWhat does **motionless** mean?","o":["very angry","hungry","fast asleep","not moving"],"a":3,"e":"The gator was so still people thought it was a statue. **Motionless** means not moving at all."},
  {"id":"voc-020","skill":"v.context","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"My little brother is **inquisitive**; he asks 'Why?' about everything from clouds to cheese.\"\n\nWhat does **inquisitive** mean?","o":["silly and full of loud jokes","grumpy and tired","curious and full of questions","quiet and shy around new people"],"a":2,"e":"Asking \"Why?\" about everything shows he wants to know more. **Inquisitive** means curious."},
  {"id":"voc-021","skill":"v.context","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"The volunteers worked to **restore** the old carousel, fixing its broken horses and painting them bright colors again.\"\n\nWhat does **restore** mean?","o":["bring back to good condition","sell to a museum","take apart and sell for pieces","move to a new place"],"a":0,"e":"Fixing and repainting are clues. To **restore** means to bring something back to good condition."},
  {"id":"voc-022","skill":"v.context","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"Lily felt **drowsy** after the long car ride, and her eyes kept closing during the movie.\"\n\nWhat does **drowsy** mean?","o":["cheerful","confused","sleepy","carsick"],"a":2,"e":"Her eyes kept closing, which is what happens when you are sleepy. **Drowsy** means sleepy."},
  {"id":"voc-023","skill":"v.context","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"The chef **combined** the flour, sugar, and eggs in one big bowl before stirring.\"\n\nWhat does **combined** mean?","o":["poured out","measured carefully","heated up","mixed together"],"a":3,"e":"Putting everything in one big bowl is a clue. **Combined** means mixed together."},
  {"id":"voc-024","skill":"v.context","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"Since the museum was **vast**, we needed a map so we would not get lost in its many halls.\"\n\nWhat does **vast** mean?","o":["very quiet","very large","very dark","very old"],"a":1,"e":"Needing a map to avoid getting lost in \"many halls\" tells you the museum was huge. **Vast** means very large."},
  {"id":"voc-025","skill":"v.context","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"Even though the puzzle was difficult, Sam was **determined** to finish it, so he kept trying until bedtime.\"\n\nWhat does **determined** mean?","o":["not sure what to do next","in a big hurry","not willing to give up","feeling bored"],"a":2,"e":"Sam kept trying until bedtime. That shows he would not give up. **Determined** means not willing to give up."},
  {"id":"voc-026","skill":"v.context","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"The pelican **plunged** into the water and came back up with a fish in its beak.\"\n\nWhat does **plunged** mean?","o":["floated slowly on top","flew away","called loudly","dove down quickly"],"a":3,"e":"The pelican went into the water and came back up with a fish, so it dove down fast."},
  {"id":"voc-027","skill":"v.context","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"Ella's answer was **accurate**; every number matched the answer key exactly.\"\n\nWhat does **accurate** mean?","o":["correct","late","messy","creative"],"a":0,"e":"Every number matched the answer key, so the answer was correct. **Accurate** means correct and exact."},
  {"id":"voc-028","skill":"v.context","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"The baby goat was very **frisky**, hopping, kicking, and running in circles around the pen.\"\n\nWhat does **frisky** mean?","o":["hungry and loud","playful and full of energy","calm and gentle with everyone","small and weak from hunger"],"a":1,"e":"Hopping, kicking, and running in circles are clues that **frisky** means playful and full of energy."},
  {"id":"voc-029","skill":"v.context","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"The class had to **postpone** the field trip because of the storm, so they will go next Friday instead.\"\n\nWhat does **postpone** mean?","o":["cancel forever","plan very carefully ahead","make shorter","move to a later time"],"a":3,"e":"\"They will go next Friday instead\" shows the trip was moved later, not canceled. That is what **postpone** means."},
  {"id":"voc-030","skill":"v.context","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"The library was so **tranquil** that the only sound was the soft turning of pages.\"\n\nWhat does **tranquil** mean?","o":["calm and quiet","messy and cluttered","busy and crowded","dark and spooky"],"a":0,"e":"The only sound was soft page-turning, so the room was calm and quiet. That is **tranquil**."},
  {"id":"voc-031","skill":"v.context","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"Marco **hesitated** at the top of the slide, unsure whether he wanted to go down.\"\n\nWhat does **hesitated** mean?","o":["jumped right away","paused because he was unsure","laughed loudly","climbed back down the ladder quickly"],"a":1,"e":"The clue \"unsure whether he wanted to go down\" shows Marco paused. **Hesitated** means paused because of being unsure."},
  {"id":"voc-032","skill":"v.context","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"The cookies were **scrumptious**, and everyone at the bake sale asked for a second one.\"\n\nWhat does **scrumptious** mean?","o":["tiny","expensive","delicious","burnt"],"a":2,"e":"People wanted seconds, which means the cookies tasted great. **Scrumptious** means delicious."},
  {"id":"voc-033","skill":"v.context","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"Keisha **soared** across the stage in a giant leap that made the audience gasp.\"\n\nWhat does **soared** mean?","o":["moved high through the air","tiptoed quietly","tripped and fell down hard","spun around in a small circle"],"a":0,"e":"A \"giant leap\" that made people gasp is a clue. **Soared** means moved high through the air."},
  {"id":"voc-034","skill":"v.context","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"The squirrel **hoarded** acorns all fall, stuffing hundreds of them into a hollow tree.\"\n\nWhat does **hoarded** mean?","o":["shared with other squirrels","dropped by accident on the ground","ate very quickly","collected and stored a lot"],"a":3,"e":"Stuffing hundreds of acorns into a tree means the squirrel was saving up a big pile. **Hoarded** means collected and stored a lot."},
  {"id":"voc-035","skill":"v.context","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"The flamingo stood on one leg, perfectly **balanced**, even as the wind blew.\"\n\nWhat does **balanced** mean?","o":["ready to fly","steady without falling","tired and weak from flying","bright pink all over"],"a":1,"e":"The flamingo did not fall even in the wind. **Balanced** means steady without falling."},
  {"id":"voc-036","skill":"v.context","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"To keep the fish healthy, we must **maintain** the tank by cleaning it every week.\"\n\nWhat does **maintain** mean?","o":["decorate","replace","take care of","empty out completely"],"a":2,"e":"Cleaning the tank every week keeps it in good shape. **Maintain** means to take care of something."},
  {"id":"voc-037","skill":"v.context","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"The sand was **scorching** at noon, so we ran to the water as fast as we could to cool our feet.\"\n\nWhat does **scorching** mean?","o":["very hot","very rough","very wet","very soft"],"a":0,"e":"We had to cool our feet in the water, so the sand must have been very hot. That is **scorching**."},
  {"id":"voc-038","skill":"v.context","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"Ben was **furious** when his sister broke his model airplane; his face turned red and he stomped out of the room.\"\n\nWhat does **furious** mean?","o":["a little upset","confused","very angry","very sad"],"a":2,"e":"A red face and stomping are signs of strong anger. **Furious** means very angry."},
  {"id":"voc-039","skill":"v.context","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"The scientist made a careful **observation** by watching the ants for an hour and writing down everything they did.\"\n\nWhat does **observation** mean?","o":["a quick guess about something","the act of watching closely","a loud noise","a type of bug that lives in dirt"],"a":1,"e":"Watching the ants and writing down what they did is the clue. An **observation** is watching something closely."},
  {"id":"voc-040","skill":"v.context","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"The new student seemed **anxious** on her first day; she kept twisting her bracelet and looking at the door.\"\n\nWhat does **anxious** mean?","o":["bored and very sleepy","angry and loud","happy and calm","worried or nervous"],"a":3,"e":"Twisting a bracelet and looking at the door are nervous habits. **Anxious** means worried or nervous."},
  {"id":"voc-041","skill":"v.context","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"The orange tree **produced** so much fruit that we gave bags of oranges to all our neighbors.\"\n\nWhat does **produced** mean?","o":["hid","made or grew","lost","needed"],"a":1,"e":"There was so much fruit to give away, so the tree grew a lot. **Produced** means made or grew."},
  {"id":"voc-042","skill":"v.context","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"The puppy was **obedient**; it sat, stayed, and rolled over every time it was told.\"\n\nWhat does **obedient** mean?","o":["small and fluffy","wild, noisy, and hard to train","sleepy and slow to wake up","doing what you are told"],"a":3,"e":"The puppy did each thing it was told to do. That is what **obedient** means."},
  {"id":"voc-043","skill":"v.context","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"The water in the spring was so **transparent** that we could see turtles swimming on the bottom.\"\n\nWhat does **transparent** mean?","o":["very deep","very cold all year round","clear enough to see through","full of plants and tiny fish"],"a":2,"e":"Being able to see turtles at the bottom means the water was clear. **Transparent** means clear enough to see through."},
  {"id":"voc-044","skill":"v.context","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"Instead of rushing, Mia worked **cautiously**, checking each step of her experiment twice.\"\n\nWhat does **cautiously** mean?","o":["carefully","quickly","loudly","sadly"],"a":0,"e":"\"Instead of rushing\" and \"checking each step twice\" are clues that she worked carefully."},
  {"id":"voc-045","skill":"v.context","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"The two cats **despise** each other; they hiss and growl whenever they are in the same room.\"\n\nWhat does **despise** mean?","o":["take good care of","strongly dislike","look like","play with"],"a":1,"e":"Hissing and growling show the cats really do not like each other. **Despise** means strongly dislike."},
  {"id":"voc-046","skill":"v.context","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"The magician made the coin **vanish**; one second it was in his hand, and the next second it was gone.\"\n\nWhat does **vanish** mean?","o":["spin","fall","disappear","shine"],"a":2,"e":"\"The next second it was gone\" tells you **vanish** means disappear."},
  {"id":"voc-047","skill":"v.context","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"The judges said Aiko's paper crane was **exquisite** because every fold was neat and delicate.\"\n\nWhat does **exquisite** mean?","o":["very beautiful and finely made","plain and simple","large, heavy, and very hard to lift","made in a big hurry"],"a":0,"e":"Neat, delicate folds and praise from judges are clues that **exquisite** means very beautiful and finely made."},
  {"id":"voc-048","skill":"v.context","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"Dad told us to **disperse** the seeds evenly across the garden, not drop them all in one spot.\"\n\nWhat does **disperse** mean?","o":["dig up","count","water","spread out"],"a":3,"e":"\"Evenly across the garden, not in one spot\" shows **disperse** means spread out."},
  {"id":"voc-049","skill":"v.context","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"The hikers were **parched** after walking in the sun, so they gulped down their water bottles.\"\n\nWhat does **parched** mean?","o":["very fast","very cold","very lost","very thirsty"],"a":3,"e":"Gulping water is what you do when you are very thirsty. **Parched** means very thirsty or very dry."},
  {"id":"voc-050","skill":"v.context","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"The crowd **dwindled** as the rain started; by the end of the game, only a few fans were left.\"\n\nWhat does **dwindled** mean?","o":["grew larger","became smaller","got louder","moved much closer"],"a":1,"e":"Only a few fans were left at the end, so the crowd got smaller. **Dwindled** means became smaller."},
  {"id":"voc-051","skill":"v.context","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"Owen is very **generous**; he shared his lunch with a friend who forgot hers and gave away half his stickers.\"\n\nWhich **two** details from the sentence help show what **generous** means? Choose **two** answers.","o":["Owen is very","shared his lunch with a friend","his stickers","gave away half his stickers","a friend who forgot hers"],"a":[1,3],"e":"Sharing lunch and giving away stickers are both acts of giving. They show that **generous** means happy to share."},
  {"id":"voc-052","skill":"v.context","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"The tortoise moved **sluggishly** across the yard, taking almost an hour to reach the lettuce leaf.\"\n\nWhich **two** words or phrases help you understand **sluggishly**? Choose **two** answers.","o":["across the yard","tortoise","lettuce leaf","moved","taking almost an hour"],"a":[3,4],"e":"\"Moved\" tells you it is about movement, and \"taking almost an hour\" tells you the movement was very slow. **Sluggishly** means slowly."},
  {"id":"voc-053","skill":"v.context","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"The storm was **ferocious**; its howling winds snapped tree branches and ripped shingles off roofs.\"\n\nWhich **two** details help you know what **ferocious** means? Choose **two** answers.","o":["howling winds","snapped tree branches","tree","The storm was","off roofs"],"a":[0,1],"e":"Howling winds that snap branches show the storm was wild and powerful. **Ferocious** means fierce and violent."},
  {"id":"voc-054","skill":"v.context","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"The ballet teacher praised Nina's **graceful** movements, saying she glided across the floor as smoothly as a swan.\"\n\nWhich **two** phrases are clues to the meaning of **graceful**? Choose **two** answers.","o":["The ballet teacher","glided across the floor","praised Nina's","saying she","as smoothly as a swan"],"a":[1,4],"e":"Gliding and moving smoothly like a swan both describe beautiful, smooth movement. That is what **graceful** means."},
  {"id":"voc-055","skill":"v.context","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"The squishy cat toy was **durable**; it survived being dropped, chewed by the dog, and even washed in the machine.\"\n\nWhich **two** details help show what **durable** means? Choose **two** answers.","o":["squishy cat toy","by the dog","survived being dropped","even washed in the machine","The squishy"],"a":[2,3],"e":"The toy survived dropping and washing without breaking. Those clues show **durable** means strong and long-lasting."},
  {"id":"voc-056","skill":"v.context","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"At the fair, the line for the roller coaster was **enormous**, stretching all the way past the lemonade stand and the petting zoo.\"\n\nWhich **two** details help you understand **enormous**? Choose **two** answers.","o":["the line","At the fair","for the roller coaster","stretching all the way","past the lemonade stand and the petting zoo"],"a":[3,4],"e":"A line that stretches past two places must be huge. **Enormous** means very, very big."},
  {"id":"voc-057","skill":"v.context","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"Rosa was **frugal** with her allowance, saving most of it and spending only a little on treats.\"\n\nWhich **two** phrases help explain **frugal**? Choose **two** answers.","o":["on treats","spending only a little","Rosa was","her allowance","saving most of it"],"a":[1,4],"e":"Saving most and spending only a little are both clues. **Frugal** means careful about spending money."},
  {"id":"voc-058","skill":"v.context","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"The cafeteria became **chaotic** when the fire drill bell rang: trays clattered, chairs scraped, and kids talked over each other.\"\n\nWhich **two** details help show what **chaotic** means? Choose **two** answers.","o":["the fire drill bell","The cafeteria became","rang","trays clattered","kids talked over each other"],"a":[3,4],"e":"Clattering trays and kids talking over each other paint a picture of a big, noisy mess. **Chaotic** means wild and out of order."},
  {"id":"voc-059","skill":"v.context","lv":4,"q":"Read the paragraph.\n\nTomas had practiced his tap routine every day for a month. On the night of the recital, he walked onstage feeling **confident**. He smiled at the audience, took a deep breath, and tapped every step without a single mistake.\n\nWhat does **confident** mean?","o":["sure of yourself","tired and sore","silly and goofy","nervous and shaky"],"a":0,"e":"Tomas practiced a lot, smiled, and did every step right. Those clues show he felt sure of himself, which is what **confident** means."},
  {"id":"voc-060","skill":"v.context","lv":4,"q":"Read the paragraph.\n\nFlorida's coral reefs are home to many fish. Sadly, some reefs have begun to **deteriorate**. The coral loses its color, pieces break off, and fewer fish live there.\n\nWhat does **deteriorate** mean?","o":["move away","grow bigger","get worse","become colorful"],"a":2,"e":"Losing color, breaking off, and having fewer fish are all signs of getting worse. **Deteriorate** means to get worse over time."},
  {"id":"voc-061","skill":"v.context","lv":5,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"The scientist was **meticulous**, recording every measurement to the tenth of a millimeter and double-checking each one.\"\n\nWhat does **meticulous** mean?","o":["quick and careless","extremely careful about details","friendly and kind to everyone around","curious about new ideas"],"a":1,"e":"Recording tiny measurements and double-checking each one shows great care with details. That is **meticulous**."},
  {"id":"voc-062","skill":"v.context","lv":5,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"Although the opening act was **mediocre**, the main dancers were so amazing that the audience gave them a standing ovation.\"\n\nWhat does **mediocre** mean?","o":["very funny","much too long","just okay, not very good","extremely exciting and fun"],"a":2,"e":"\"Although\" signals a contrast. The main dancers were amazing, so the opening act was the opposite: only so-so. **Mediocre** means just okay."},
  {"id":"voc-063","skill":"v.context","lv":5,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"The explorers found the cave entrance **obscured** by thick vines, so they almost walked right past it.\"\n\nWhat does **obscured** mean?","o":["hidden from view","blocked by rocks","clearly marked with signs","made larger"],"a":0,"e":"They almost walked past it because vines covered it. **Obscured** means hidden from view."},
  {"id":"voc-064","skill":"v.context","lv":5,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"Instead of giving a straight answer, the politician's reply was **ambiguous**, and reporters argued about what he meant.\"\n\nWhat does **ambiguous** mean?","o":["completely wrong","carefully planned and written ahead of time","spoken very loudly and quickly into a microphone","having more than one possible meaning"],"a":3,"e":"\"Instead of a straight answer\" and people arguing about the meaning show it was unclear. **Ambiguous** means it could mean more than one thing."},
  {"id":"voc-065","skill":"v.context","lv":5,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"The drought was so **prolonged** that the lake dropped lower month after month for almost two years.\"\n\nWhat does **prolonged** mean?","o":["lasting a long time","extremely hot and sunny","very sudden","easy to fix"],"a":0,"e":"\"Month after month for almost two years\" is the clue. **Prolonged** means lasting a long time."},
  {"id":"voc-066","skill":"v.context","lv":5,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"The coach **commended** the players for their teamwork, saying she had never been prouder of a group.\"\n\nWhat does **commended** mean?","o":["tested","surprised","scolded","praised"],"a":3,"e":"The coach said she had never been prouder, so she was praising them. **Commended** means praised."},
  {"id":"voc-067","skill":"v.context","lv":5,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"Pablo **scrutinized** the map, studying every line and symbol before choosing which trail to take.\"\n\nWhat does **scrutinized** mean?","o":["drew quickly","looked at very closely","threw away","folded up neatly into a square"],"a":1,"e":"\"Studying every line and symbol\" is the clue. **Scrutinized** means looked at very closely."},
  {"id":"voc-068","skill":"v.context","lv":5,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"Even the most **skeptical** students began to believe the plan would work when they saw the test results.\"\n\nWhat does **skeptical** mean?","o":["tired of waiting for an answer","cheerful and positive about everything","doubting or not easily convinced","shy and quiet"],"a":2,"e":"\"Even the most skeptical... began to believe\" means they did not believe it before. **Skeptical** means doubtful."},
  {"id":"voc-069","skill":"v.context","lv":5,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"The tiny tree frog is **inconspicuous** on a green leaf; its color blends in so well that predators rarely notice it.\"\n\nWhat does **inconspicuous** mean?","o":["not easily seen","slow-moving","very loud","brightly colored"],"a":0,"e":"The frog blends in and predators rarely notice it. **Inconspicuous** means not easily seen."},
  {"id":"voc-070","skill":"v.context","lv":5,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"After weeks of **tedious** practice drills, the band was thrilled to finally play a fun new song.\"\n\nWhat does **tedious** mean?","o":["loud and fast","short and easy","exciting and brand new","boring and repetitive"],"a":3,"e":"The band was thrilled to finally play something fun, so the drills were the opposite: boring. **Tedious** means boring and repetitive."},
  {"id":"voc-071","skill":"v.context","lv":5,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"The mayor hoped to **alleviate** traffic near the school by adding a new crosswalk and a second drop-off lane.\"\n\nWhat does **alleviate** mean?","o":["leave alone","make much worse","make less severe","study carefully"],"a":2,"e":"A crosswalk and an extra lane would help traffic. **Alleviate** means to make a problem less severe."},
  {"id":"voc-072","skill":"v.context","lv":5,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"The museum's collection of seashells was **diverse**, with tiny pink shells, huge spiral conchs, and striped scallops.\"\n\nWhat does **diverse** mean?","o":["very old and rare","having many different kinds","all the same size, shape, and color","expensive"],"a":1,"e":"The list of very different shells is the clue. **Diverse** means having many different kinds."},
  {"id":"voc-073","skill":"v.context","lv":5,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"The old lighthouse keeper lived a **solitary** life, spending months on the island with no one but his cat.\"\n\nWhat does **solitary** mean?","o":["alone","exciting","difficult","busy"],"a":0,"e":"Living for months with no one but a cat means he was alone. **Solitary** means alone."},
  {"id":"voc-074","skill":"v.context","lv":5,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"The young inventor was **persistent**; after her robot failed twelve times, she rebuilt it a thirteenth time and finally succeeded.\"\n\nWhich **two** details best help show what **persistent** means? Choose **two** answers.","o":["after her robot failed twelve times","her robot","finally","she rebuilt it a thirteenth time","The young inventor"],"a":[0,3],"e":"Failing twelve times and still rebuilding shows she kept going. **Persistent** means continuing even when things are hard."},
  {"id":"voc-075","skill":"v.context","lv":5,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"The museum guide spoke with such **enthusiasm** about dinosaurs that her eyes lit up and she waved her arms as she talked.\"\n\nWhich **two** details help you understand **enthusiasm**? Choose **two** answers.","o":["The museum guide","spoke with","about dinosaurs","she waved her arms as she talked","her eyes lit up"],"a":[3,4],"e":"Lit-up eyes and waving arms show strong excitement. **Enthusiasm** means great excitement and interest."},
  {"id":"voc-076","skill":"v.roots","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"Kenji was **unhappy** when the rain canceled his soccer game.\"\n\nThe prefix **un-** means \"not.\" What does **unhappy** mean?","o":["happy again","very happy","happy before","not happy"],"a":3,"e":"**Un-** means \"not,\" so **unhappy** means not happy."},
  {"id":"voc-077","skill":"v.roots","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"Mom asked me to **reheat** the leftover rice for lunch.\"\n\nWhat does **reheat** mean?","o":["heat too much","heat again","not heat","heat before"],"a":1,"e":"The prefix **re-** means \"again.\" To **reheat** is to heat again."},
  {"id":"voc-078","skill":"v.roots","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"My cat seems to **dislike** baths; she runs every time she hears the water.\"\n\nWhat does the prefix **dis-** mean in **dislike**?","o":["back again or one more time","before","not or the opposite of","under or below"],"a":2,"e":"**Dis-** means \"not\" or \"the opposite of.\" **Dislike** is the opposite of like."},
  {"id":"voc-079","skill":"v.roots","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"Our class took a **pretest** on fractions before we started the new unit.\"\n\nWhat does **pretest** mean?","o":["a test given before","a test that is not graded","a test that is wrong","a test given again"],"a":0,"e":"**Pre-** means \"before.\" A **pretest** is a test you take before learning something."},
  {"id":"voc-080","skill":"v.roots","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"Lucas **misspelled** 'Florida' on his poster, so he had to fix it.\"\n\nWhat does **misspelled** mean?","o":["spelled again","spelled neatly","spelled before","spelled wrongly"],"a":3,"e":"**Mis-** means \"wrongly\" or \"badly.\" **Misspelled** means spelled wrongly."},
  {"id":"voc-081","skill":"v.roots","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"The flight from Tampa to New York was **nonstop**, so we did not land anywhere in between.\"\n\nWhat does **nonstop** mean?","o":["stopping early","stopping often","without stopping","stopping again"],"a":2,"e":"**Non-** means \"not\" or \"without.\" A **nonstop** flight goes without stopping."},
  {"id":"voc-082","skill":"v.roots","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"Riding a bike without a helmet is **unsafe**.\"\n\nWhich word has a prefix that means the same as the **un-** in **unsafe**?","o":["rewrite","nonfiction","preview","subway"],"a":1,"e":"**Un-** and **non-** both mean \"not.\" **Nonfiction** means not made up."},
  {"id":"voc-083","skill":"v.roots","lv":4,"q":"The word **submarine** has the prefix **sub-**. What does **sub-** mean?","o":["over or above","again","under or below","across"],"a":2,"e":"**Sub-** means \"under.\" A **submarine** travels under the sea."},
  {"id":"voc-084","skill":"v.roots","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"The highway system includes **interstate** roads that connect Florida to Georgia and Alabama.\"\n\nWhat does the prefix **inter-** mean in **interstate**?","o":["inside","not","before","between"],"a":3,"e":"**Inter-** means \"between.\" **Interstate** roads go between states."},
  {"id":"voc-085","skill":"v.roots","lv":4,"q":"A **tricycle** has three wheels. What does the prefix **tri-** mean?","o":["many","three","two","small"],"a":1,"e":"**Tri-** means \"three,\" like in **triangle** (three angles) and **tricycle** (three wheels)."},
  {"id":"voc-086","skill":"v.roots","lv":4,"q":"Which word means \"happening every two years\"?","o":["biennial","annual","centennial","triennial"],"a":0,"e":"**Bi-** means \"two.\" So **biennial** means every two years."},
  {"id":"voc-087","skill":"v.roots","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"The puppy was **playful** all afternoon, chasing balls and tugging on ropes.\"\n\nWhat does the suffix **-ful** mean in **playful**?","o":["one who","full of","able to be","without"],"a":1,"e":"**-ful** means \"full of.\" **Playful** means full of play."},
  {"id":"voc-088","skill":"v.roots","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"The broken toaster was **useless**, so we bought a new one.\"\n\nWhat does **useless** mean?","o":["without use","full of use","used again","used before"],"a":0,"e":"**-less** means \"without.\" **Useless** means without any use."},
  {"id":"voc-089","skill":"v.roots","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"The squishy toys came in a **washable** fabric, so Mom tossed them in with the laundry.\"\n\nWhat does **washable** mean?","o":["without washing","full of water","able to be washed","washed before"],"a":2,"e":"**-able** means \"able to be.\" **Washable** means able to be washed."},
  {"id":"voc-090","skill":"v.roots","lv":4,"q":"A **teacher** is a person who teaches. Which word uses the same suffix meaning \"one who\"?","o":["danced","dancing","dances","dancer"],"a":3,"e":"**-er** can mean \"one who.\" A **dancer** is one who dances."},
  {"id":"voc-091","skill":"v.roots","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"Everyone noticed the **kindness** Maria showed when she helped the new student find her classes.\"\n\nWhat does the suffix **-ness** do to the word **kind**?","o":["It makes it mean \"not kind.\"","It turns it into a word that names a quality.","It makes it mean \"kind again.\"","It turns it into a word that names an action you do."],"a":1,"e":"**-ness** means \"the state or quality of.\" **Kindness** names the quality of being kind."},
  {"id":"voc-092","skill":"v.roots","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"The class shouted with **excitement** when they heard about the field trip to the aquarium.\"\n\nThe suffix **-ment** means \"the act or state of.\" What does **excitement** mean?","o":["able to excite","one who excites","the state of being excited","without any excitement at all"],"a":2,"e":"**-ment** makes a word that names a state. **Excitement** is the state of being excited."},
  {"id":"voc-093","skill":"v.roots","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"The cat crept **quietly** toward the toy mouse.\"\n\nWhat does the suffix **-ly** tell you about **quietly**?","o":["It tells how something was done.","It tells that something is full of quiet.","It tells who did something.","It tells when something happened."],"a":0,"e":"**-ly** often means \"in a way that is.\" **Quietly** tells how the cat crept: in a quiet way."},
  {"id":"voc-094","skill":"v.roots","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"Climbing the slippery rocks near the waterfall was **dangerous**.\"\n\nWhat does the suffix **-ous** mean in **dangerous**?","o":["before","again","without","full of"],"a":3,"e":"**-ous** means \"full of\" or \"having.\" **Dangerous** means full of danger."},
  {"id":"voc-095","skill":"v.roots","lv":4,"q":"The root **port** means \"carry.\" Which word means \"able to be carried\"?","o":["porter","portable","report","import"],"a":1,"e":"**Port** means \"carry\" and **-able** means \"able to be,\" so **portable** means able to be carried."},
  {"id":"voc-096","skill":"v.roots","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"Please do not **interrupt** your sister while she is talking.\"\n\nThe root **rupt** means \"break.\" What does **interrupt** mean?","o":["talk very loudly to a group","repeat what someone said","break in between someone's talking","agree with everything someone is saying"],"a":2,"e":"**Inter-** means \"between\" and **rupt** means \"break.\" To **interrupt** is to break in while someone is talking."},
  {"id":"voc-097","skill":"v.roots","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"The teacher read each word aloud for the spelling **dictation**.\"\n\nThe root **dict** means \"say\" or \"speak.\" Which word also has this root?","o":["predict","direct","picture","distance"],"a":0,"e":"**Predict** has the root **dict**. It means to say what will happen before it happens."},
  {"id":"voc-098","skill":"v.roots","lv":4,"q":"The root **graph** means \"write\" or \"draw.\" What is an **autograph**?","o":["a type of map","a drawing of a car","a song you sing about yourself","a person's own written name"],"a":3,"e":"**Auto** means \"self\" and **graph** means \"write.\" An **autograph** is your name written by you."},
  {"id":"voc-099","skill":"v.roots","lv":4,"q":"The root **tele** means \"far.\" Which word means \"an instrument for seeing faraway things\"?","o":["telegraph","television","telephone","telescope"],"a":3,"e":"**Tele** means \"far\" and **scope** means \"see.\" A **telescope** helps you see faraway things like the moon."},
  {"id":"voc-100","skill":"v.roots","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"Mrs. Lee asked us to **inscribe** our names on the inside cover of our journals.\"\n\nThe root **scrib** means \"write.\" What does **inscribe** mean?","o":["write on or in something","erase something","draw a picture","read something aloud to the class"],"a":0,"e":"**Scrib** means \"write,\" so **inscribe** means to write on or in something."},
  {"id":"voc-101","skill":"v.roots","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"The builders spent months on the **construction** of the new school gym.\"\n\nThe root **struct** means \"build.\" What does **construction** mean?","o":["the act of planning","the act of building","the act of tearing down","the act of painting"],"a":1,"e":"**Struct** means \"build,\" so **construction** is the act of building."},
  {"id":"voc-102","skill":"v.roots","lv":4,"q":"The root **vis** means \"see.\" Which word means \"able to be seen\"?","o":["visitor","vision","visible","revise"],"a":2,"e":"**Vis** means \"see\" and **-ible** means \"able to be.\" **Visible** means able to be seen."},
  {"id":"voc-103","skill":"v.roots","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"The **audience** clapped loudly after the dance recital.\"\n\nThe root **aud** means \"hear.\" Why does **audience** make sense for the people at a show?","o":["They are the people who perform.","They are the people who listen and watch.","They are the people who sell tickets.","They are the people who build and paint the stage."],"a":1,"e":"**Aud** means \"hear.\" The **audience** is the group that hears and watches the show."},
  {"id":"voc-104","skill":"v.roots","lv":4,"q":"The root **phon** means \"sound.\" Which word is about sound?","o":["geography","photograph","microphone","biology"],"a":2,"e":"**Microphone** has **phon**. It is a tool that makes sound louder."},
  {"id":"voc-105","skill":"v.roots","lv":4,"q":"The root **bio** means \"life.\" What does a **biologist** study?","o":["living things","rocks","the weather","numbers"],"a":0,"e":"**Bio** means \"life,\" so a **biologist** studies living things."},
  {"id":"voc-106","skill":"v.roots","lv":4,"q":"The root **geo** means \"earth.\" Which subject is about the earth and its land, water, and places?","o":["biography","photography","telegraph","geography"],"a":3,"e":"**Geo** means \"earth\" and **graph** means \"write.\" **Geography** is writing about or describing the earth."},
  {"id":"voc-107","skill":"v.roots","lv":4,"q":"The root **micro** means \"small.\" What does a **microscope** help you see?","o":["very tiny things","loud sounds","faraway stars","the future"],"a":0,"e":"**Micro** means \"small\" and **scope** means \"see.\" A **microscope** helps you see tiny things."},
  {"id":"voc-108","skill":"v.roots","lv":4,"q":"The root **photo** means \"light.\" Plants use **photosynthesis** to make food. Which part of photosynthesis does **photo** point to?","o":["the color of the leaves","the air around the plant","the water from the soil","the light from the sun"],"a":3,"e":"**Photo** means \"light.\" Plants use sunlight to make their food."},
  {"id":"voc-109","skill":"v.roots","lv":4,"q":"The root **therm** means \"heat.\" Which tool measures how hot or cold something is?","o":["speedometer","thermometer","microphone","telescope"],"a":1,"e":"**Therm** means \"heat\" and **meter** means \"measure.\" A **thermometer** measures heat."},
  {"id":"voc-110","skill":"v.roots","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"We visited an **aquarium** in Tampa and saw sea turtles and sharks.\"\n\nThe root **aqua** means \"water.\" Why is **aquarium** a good name for this place?","o":["It is a place that sells fish food and bowls.","It is a place with lots of plants.","It is a place where water animals live.","It is a place where you can swim."],"a":2,"e":"**Aqua** means \"water.\" An **aquarium** is a place that holds water animals."},
  {"id":"voc-111","skill":"v.roots","lv":4,"q":"The root **terr** means \"land\" or \"earth.\" Which word names a place where land animals or plants are kept?","o":["aquarium","auditorium","terrarium","planetarium"],"a":2,"e":"**Terr** means \"land.\" A **terrarium** is a container for land plants or animals."},
  {"id":"voc-112","skill":"v.roots","lv":4,"q":"The root **meter** means \"measure.\" What does a **speedometer** do?","o":["makes a car go faster","measures how fast something goes","shows which roads to drive on to get home","measures the temperature"],"a":1,"e":"**Meter** means \"measure,\" so a **speedometer** measures speed."},
  {"id":"voc-113","skill":"v.roots","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"The coach said his decision was **unchangeable**.\"\n\nWhich meaning best fits **unchangeable**?","o":["changed again","full of lots of different changes","one who changes","not able to be changed"],"a":3,"e":"**Un-** means \"not,\" **change** is the base, and **-able** means \"able to be.\" So it means not able to be changed."},
  {"id":"voc-114","skill":"v.roots","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"The magician's trick seemed **impossible** to explain.\"\n\nWhat does the prefix **im-** mean in **impossible**?","o":["not","inside","again","before"],"a":0,"e":"**Im-** is another form of **in-**, and it can mean \"not.\" **Impossible** means not possible."},
  {"id":"voc-115","skill":"v.roots","lv":4,"q":"Which **two** words have a prefix that means \"again\"? Choose **two** answers.","o":["replay","really","reach","rebuild","ready"],"a":[0,3],"e":"**Rebuild** and **replay** mean build again and play again. In **reach**, **ready**, and **really**, the letters \"re\" are not a prefix."},
  {"id":"voc-116","skill":"v.roots","lv":4,"q":"Which **two** words have a suffix that means \"without\"? Choose **two** answers.","o":["careful","fearless","lesson","careless","bless"],"a":[1,3],"e":"**-less** means \"without.\" **Fearless** means without fear, and **careless** means without care."},
  {"id":"voc-117","skill":"v.roots","lv":4,"q":"Which **two** words have a root that means \"see\" or \"look\"? Choose **two** answers.","o":["video","audio","deport","inject","inspect"],"a":[0,4],"e":"**Spect** means \"look\" and **vid** means \"see.\" **Inspect** and **video** both have to do with seeing."},
  {"id":"voc-118","skill":"v.roots","lv":4,"q":"Which **two** words have a root that means \"write\"? Choose **two** answers.","o":["telephone","paragraph","structure","scribble","export"],"a":[1,3],"e":"**Graph** and **scrib** both mean \"write.\" A **paragraph** is written, and to **scribble** is to write messily."},
  {"id":"voc-119","skill":"v.roots","lv":4,"q":"Which **two** words name a person who does something? Choose **two** answers.","o":["inventor","beautiful","sadness","swimmer","quickly"],"a":[0,3],"e":"**-or** and **-er** can mean \"one who.\" An **inventor** invents, and a **swimmer** swims."},
  {"id":"voc-120","skill":"v.roots","lv":4,"q":"Which **two** words have a prefix that means \"two\"? Choose **two** answers.","o":["triangle","bicycle","binoculars","tripod","bingo"],"a":[1,2],"e":"**Bi-** means \"two.\" A **bicycle** has two wheels and **binoculars** have two eyepieces. **Bingo** just starts with \"bi.\""},
  {"id":"voc-121","skill":"v.roots","lv":5,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"The tour guide told us the old fort's walls were **impenetrable** to cannonballs.\"\n\nUsing the prefix **im-** and the suffix **-able**, what does **impenetrable** mean?","o":["built again and again","not able to be gotten through","easy for anyone at all to break into","covered with paint"],"a":1,"e":"**Im-** means \"not,\" **penetrate** means \"get through,\" and **-able** means \"able to be.\" So it means not able to be gotten through."},
  {"id":"voc-122","skill":"v.roots","lv":5,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"The scientist used a **hydrometer** to check the water in the experiment.\"\n\nThe root **hydro** means \"water\" and **meter** means \"measure.\" What does a **hydrometer** do?","o":["heats water quickly","cleans the dirty water in a fish pond","makes waves in a pool of water","measures something about a liquid"],"a":3,"e":"**Hydro** is water and **meter** is measure, so a **hydrometer** measures something about water or other liquids."},
  {"id":"voc-123","skill":"v.roots","lv":5,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"The class debated whether the rule was **reversible** or permanent.\"\n\nThe root **vers** means \"turn.\" What does **reversible** mean?","o":["able to be turned back or undone","turned upside down and shaken hard","broken into pieces","not able to change at all"],"a":0,"e":"**Re-** means \"back,\" **vers** means \"turn,\" and **-ible** means \"able to be.\" **Reversible** means able to be turned back or undone."},
  {"id":"voc-124","skill":"v.roots","lv":5,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"The hiking group was **disheartened** when they reached the top and found the view covered in fog.\"\n\nWhat does **disheartened** mean?","o":["very surprised","having a strong heart","having lost hope or cheer","full of energy"],"a":2,"e":"**Dis-** means \"the opposite of.\" Losing heart means losing hope or cheer. Seeing only fog would be a letdown."},
  {"id":"voc-125","skill":"v.roots","lv":5,"q":"The root **chron** means \"time.\" Which word means \"arranged in the order things happened in time\"?","o":["synchronize","chronicle","chronic","chronological"],"a":3,"e":"**Chron** means \"time.\" **Chronological** order puts events in time order, from first to last."},
  {"id":"voc-126","skill":"v.roots","lv":5,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"The ranger said the swamp was **inhospitable** to most kinds of birds that need dry nesting spots.\"\n\nWhat does **inhospitable** mean?","o":["not welcoming or easy to live in","full of hospitals","warm, friendly, and very comfortable","very crowded with many animals"],"a":0,"e":"**In-** means \"not,\" and **hospitable** means welcoming. A place that is **inhospitable** is not welcoming or easy to live in."},
  {"id":"voc-127","skill":"v.roots","lv":5,"q":"The root **cred** means \"believe.\" Which word means \"hard to believe\"?","o":["creed","incredible","credential","credit"],"a":1,"e":"**In-** means \"not,\" **cred** means \"believe,\" and **-ible** means \"able to be.\" **Incredible** means not able to be believed, or amazing."},
  {"id":"voc-128","skill":"v.roots","lv":5,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"Our town's population has grown so much that the old library is now **inadequate**.\"\n\nWhat does **inadequate** mean?","o":["built long ago","full of books and quiet spaces","not enough or not good enough","far away"],"a":2,"e":"**In-** means \"not,\" and **adequate** means \"enough.\" So **inadequate** means not enough for the job."},
  {"id":"voc-129","skill":"v.roots","lv":5,"q":"The root **mot** or **mob** means \"move.\" Which word does NOT come from this root?","o":["motion","mobile","motor","motto"],"a":3,"e":"**Motion**, **mobile**, and **motor** are all about moving. A **motto** is a saying, so it does not come from this root."},
  {"id":"voc-130","skill":"v.roots","lv":5,"q":"Which **two** words come from Greek or Latin parts that both mean \"sound\" or \"hear\"? Choose **two** answers.","o":["auditorium","geology","autograph","symphony","spectator"],"a":[0,3],"e":"**Phon** means \"sound\" and **aud** means \"hear.\" A **symphony** is music, and an **auditorium** is a place to listen."},
  {"id":"voc-131","skill":"v.multi","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"A tiny brown **bat** flew out of the barn at sunset.\"\n\nWhat does **bat** mean in this sentence?","o":["a small flying animal","a stick used to hit a ball","to hit something","to blink your eyes"],"a":0,"e":"The bat \"flew out of the barn,\" so it must be the flying animal, not the baseball stick."},
  {"id":"voc-132","skill":"v.multi","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"Grandpa poured lemonade from a glass **pitcher** into our cups.\"\n\nWhat does **pitcher** mean in this sentence?","o":["a type of plant","a baseball player who throws the ball","a container for pouring liquids","a picture on a wall"],"a":2,"e":"Grandpa \"poured lemonade\" from it, so it is a container for liquids."},
  {"id":"voc-133","skill":"v.multi","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"The dancers stood in a straight **line** across the front of the stage.\"\n\nIn which sentence does **line** mean the same thing?","o":["Dad tied a new fishing line to the end of his pole.","We waited in a long line for the water slide.","Draw a line under the title.","I forgot my line in the school play."],"a":1,"e":"In both sentences, a **line** is a row of people standing one behind or next to another."},
  {"id":"voc-134","skill":"v.multi","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"Please **park** your bike next to the fence.\"\n\nWhat does **park** mean in this sentence?","o":["a place to watch baseball","to leave something in a place","to walk quickly","a green space with trees and swings"],"a":1,"e":"You \"park your bike next to the fence,\" so here **park** is an action: leaving something in a spot."},
  {"id":"voc-135","skill":"v.multi","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"The waves crashed against the rocky **bank** of the river.\"\n\nWhat does **bank** mean in this sentence?","o":["to tilt an airplane while turning","a place that keeps people's money safe","the land along the side of a river","a pile of snow"],"a":2,"e":"The waves crash against it at the river, so **bank** means the land along the river's edge."},
  {"id":"voc-136","skill":"v.multi","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"Be careful, the soup is very hot, so let it **cool** for a minute.\"\n\nIn which sentence does **cool** mean the same thing?","o":["Put the cookies on a rack to cool.","Stay cool and calm during the test.","My brother thinks he is too cool to hold my hand.","That new dance move is so cool!"],"a":0,"e":"In both sentences, **cool** means to become less hot."},
  {"id":"voc-137","skill":"v.multi","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"Leah used a **ruler** to measure the length of her squishy toy.\"\n\nWhat does **ruler** mean in this sentence?","o":["a person who makes rules for others","a king or queen","a type of pencil","a straight tool for measuring"],"a":3,"e":"She used it \"to measure,\" so it is the measuring tool."},
  {"id":"voc-138","skill":"v.multi","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"Dad will **fly** to Orlando for a meeting next week.\"\n\nIn which sentence does **fly** have the same meaning?","o":["We will fly across the ocean to visit my aunt.","The baseball was a high fly ball out to center field.","My fly fishing lure is bright red.","A fly buzzed around the picnic table."],"a":0,"e":"In both sentences, **fly** means to travel in an airplane."},
  {"id":"voc-139","skill":"v.multi","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"My little sister cried when she scraped her **palm** on the sidewalk.\"\n\nWhat does **palm** mean in this sentence?","o":["a type of beach towel","the inside of the hand","to hide something in your hand","a tall tree with big leaves"],"a":1,"e":"Scraping it on the sidewalk and crying means it is a part of her body: the inside of her hand."},
  {"id":"voc-140","skill":"v.multi","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"The judge will **present** the trophy to the winning team.\"\n\nWhat does **present** mean in this sentence?","o":["here right now","a gift","the time happening now","to give"],"a":3,"e":"The judge will hand over the trophy, so **present** means to give."},
  {"id":"voc-141","skill":"v.multi","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"Can you **tie** a bow on the gift box?\"\n\nIn which sentence does **tie** mean the same thing?","o":["Dad wore a striped tie to the wedding.","The game ended in a tie.","Tie your shoes before you run.","The two runners tie for first place."],"a":2,"e":"In both sentences, **tie** means to fasten something with a knot or bow."},
  {"id":"voc-142","skill":"v.multi","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"The **bark** on the oak tree was rough and full of tiny bugs.\"\n\nWhat does **bark** mean in this sentence?","o":["to shout an order","the sound a dog makes when it is excited","a kind of boat","the outer covering of a tree"],"a":3,"e":"The bark is \"on the oak tree,\" so it is the tree's outer covering."},
  {"id":"voc-143","skill":"v.multi","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"The baby **rose** from her nap, rubbing her eyes.\"\n\nWhat does **rose** mean in this sentence?","o":["a red flower","went higher in price","got up","a pink color"],"a":2,"e":"The baby is waking up from a nap, so **rose** means got up."},
  {"id":"voc-144","skill":"v.multi","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"Our coach wants us to **train** every afternoon before the big race.\"\n\nIn which sentence does **train** have the same meaning?","o":["We rode the train to the city.","It takes months to train a guide dog.","The train whistled as it left the station.","The bride's dress had a long train."],"a":1,"e":"In both sentences, **train** means to practice or teach a skill."},
  {"id":"voc-145","skill":"v.multi","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"Please **file** your papers in the blue folder.\"\n\nWhat does **file** mean in this sentence?","o":["to put in order and store","a tool for smoothing nails","a computer document","to walk in a line"],"a":0,"e":"You put papers in a folder, so **file** means to put in order and store."},
  {"id":"voc-146","skill":"v.multi","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"The crowd began to **cheer** when the dancers took a bow.\"\n\nIn which sentence is **bow** used the same way as in the sentence above?","o":["The bow of the big ship cut through the ocean waves.","The actor took a bow at the end of the play.","Lin put a red bow in her hair.","The archer pulled back the bow."],"a":1,"e":"In both sentences, a **bow** is bending forward to thank an audience."},
  {"id":"voc-147","skill":"v.multi","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"I could not **bear** to watch the scary part of the movie.\"\n\nWhat does **bear** mean in this sentence?","o":["to put up with","empty or bare","a large furry animal","to carry something"],"a":0,"e":"The speaker could not stand to watch, so **bear** means to put up with."},
  {"id":"voc-148","skill":"v.multi","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"Jada wrote a **note** to thank her grandma for the birthday gift.\"\n\nIn which sentence does **note** mean the same thing?","o":["The singer hit a very high note.","The piano note sounded off-key.","Mom left a note on the fridge.","Please note that the store closes early."],"a":2,"e":"In both sentences, a **note** is a short written message."},
  {"id":"voc-149","skill":"v.multi","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"We saw the **light** flash on the lighthouse every few seconds.\"\n\nWhat does **light** mean in this sentence?","o":["to start a small fire with a match","not heavy","pale in color, like a pastel","a brightness that helps you see"],"a":3,"e":"A lighthouse shines a brightness so ships can see. Here **light** means brightness."},
  {"id":"voc-150","skill":"v.multi","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"The squishy toy is so **light** that it floats in the bathtub.\"\n\nIn which sentence does **light** have the same meaning?","o":["We will light the candles on the cake.","My backpack is light today because I have no books.","Turn on the light in the den so we can read our books.","Her dress was light blue."],"a":1,"e":"In both sentences, **light** means not heavy."},
  {"id":"voc-151","skill":"v.multi","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"The coach told us to **space** out across the field so we would not crash.\"\n\nWhat does **space** mean in this sentence?","o":["to leave room between things","the area beyond Earth where stars are","the bar on a keyboard","an empty seat"],"a":0,"e":"Spreading out \"so we would not crash\" means leaving room between players."},
  {"id":"voc-152","skill":"v.multi","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"The goldfish swam around the **bowl** in slow circles.\"\n\nIn which sentence does **bowl** mean something different?","o":["Pour the cereal into the bowl.","She mixed the batter in a big bowl.","We like to bowl on Friday nights.","The salad bowl was empty."],"a":2,"e":"In the correct answer, **bowl** means to play a game where you roll a ball at pins. In the others, it means a round dish."},
  {"id":"voc-153","skill":"v.multi","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"Ms. Gomez asked the class to **match** each word with its meaning.\"\n\nWhat does **match** mean in this sentence?","o":["a sports game","a small wooden stick used to light a fire or candle","to be the same color","to put things together that go together"],"a":3,"e":"Putting each word with its meaning is pairing things that go together."},
  {"id":"voc-154","skill":"v.multi","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"The ice on the pond was so **thin** that no one was allowed to skate.\"\n\nWhat does **thin** mean in this sentence?","o":["not crowded","not thick","skinny","watery"],"a":1,"e":"Ice that is not thick can break. Here **thin** means not thick."},
  {"id":"voc-155","skill":"v.multi","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"The pelicans settled on the **pier** to rest in the sun.\"\n\nWhat does **pier** mean in this sentence?","o":["a long platform that stretches over water","to look closely","a person who is the same age and grade as you","a tall stone tower near the beach"],"a":0,"e":"Pelicans rest by the water, so **pier** means a platform over the water. (**Peer**, which sounds the same, means to look closely.)"},
  {"id":"voc-156","skill":"v.multi","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"Our class will **check** the garden every morning for new sprouts.\"\n\nIn which sentence does **check** have the same meaning?","o":["Put a check mark next to each correct answer below.","The waiter brought us the check.","Mom wrote a check to pay the plumber.","Check the oven to see if the bread is done."],"a":3,"e":"In both sentences, **check** means to look at something to find out about it."},
  {"id":"voc-157","skill":"v.multi","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"The fish **scale** shimmered silver in the sunlight.\"\n\nWhat does **scale** mean in this sentence?","o":["a set of musical notes","a tool for weighing fruit and other things in a store","a small plate that covers a fish's skin","to climb up something tall and steep"],"a":2,"e":"It is a \"fish scale\" that shimmers, so it is a small plate on a fish's skin."},
  {"id":"voc-158","skill":"v.multi","lv":4,"q":"Which **two** sentences use **pound** to mean \"to hit hard over and over\"? Choose **two** answers.","o":["The puppy came from the animal pound.","The chef had to pound the meat flat.","The cake recipe needs one pound of butter.","Grandma bought a pound of grapes.","Rain began to pound on the roof."],"a":[1,4],"e":"Rain hitting the roof and the chef flattening meat both show hitting hard again and again."},
  {"id":"voc-159","skill":"v.multi","lv":4,"q":"Which **two** sentences use **wave** to mean \"move your hand back and forth\"? Choose **two** answers.","o":["A huge wave knocked down my sandcastle.","Wave goodbye to your cousins!","A heat wave hit Florida in June.","The surfer rode the wave to shore.","The fans wave at the parade float."],"a":[1,4],"e":"Waving goodbye and fans waving at a float are both hand motions."},
  {"id":"voc-160","skill":"v.multi","lv":4,"q":"Which **two** sentences use **fair** to mean \"equal and right for everyone\"? Choose **two** answers.","o":["Her skin is very fair, so she wears sunscreen.","The weather will be fair and sunny.","The referee made a fair call.","It is only fair that everyone gets a turn.","We rode the Ferris wheel at the state fair."],"a":[2,3],"e":"Giving everyone a turn and making a fair call are both about being equal and right."},
  {"id":"voc-161","skill":"v.multi","lv":5,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"The scientist will **conduct** an experiment to see how fast ice melts in salt water.\"\n\nWhat does **conduct** mean in this sentence?","o":["to carry out or do","to lead an orchestra","the way a person behaves","to let heat pass through"],"a":0,"e":"An experiment is something you carry out, so **conduct** means to do or carry out."},
  {"id":"voc-162","skill":"v.multi","lv":5,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"The new rule had a big **impact** on how long recess lasted.\"\n\nIn which sentence does **impact** have the same meaning?","o":["The impact of the ball knocked over the cup.","The meteor's impact made a giant crater.","The car's impact with the pole bent the front bumper badly.","The rainy weather had an impact on our picnic plans."],"a":3,"e":"In both sentences, **impact** means an effect on something, not a crash."},
  {"id":"voc-163","skill":"v.multi","lv":5,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"My uncle needs to **renew** his driver's license before it runs out.\"\n\nWhich meaning of **renew** fits this sentence?","o":["to start doing something again after a break","to make something look shiny and brand new again","to make something good for a longer time","to replace something old"],"a":2,"e":"A license \"runs out,\" so to **renew** it means to make it good for a longer time."},
  {"id":"voc-164","skill":"v.multi","lv":5,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"The tide is starting to **ebb**, so the beach is getting wider.\"\n\nWhat does **ebb** mean in this sentence?","o":["turn colder","flow back away from the shore","crash loudly against the rocks","rise higher up the beach"],"a":1,"e":"The beach is getting wider, which happens when water moves away from shore."},
  {"id":"voc-165","skill":"v.multi","lv":5,"q":"Which **two** sentences use **draft** to mean \"an early version of writing\"? Choose **two** answers.","o":["The team will draft new players in the spring.","She wrote three drafts before her essay was ready.","My dog likes to nap in the cool draft from the fan.","My first draft had lots of spelling mistakes.","Close the window; there is a cold draft."],"a":[1,3],"e":"A first draft and three drafts of an essay are early versions of writing. A cold **draft** is air, and to **draft** players is to choose them."},
  {"id":"voc-166","skill":"v.relations","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"The kitten was very **tiny**.\"\n\nWhich word is a synonym for **tiny**?","o":["huge","small","cute","soft"],"a":1,"e":"A synonym means the same thing. **Tiny** and **small** both describe something little."},
  {"id":"voc-167","skill":"v.relations","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"The hallway was **noisy** after the bell rang.\"\n\nWhich word is an antonym for **noisy**?","o":["quiet","busy","crowded","loud"],"a":0,"e":"An antonym means the opposite. The opposite of **noisy** is **quiet**."},
  {"id":"voc-168","skill":"v.relations","lv":4,"q":"Which word shows the STRONGEST feeling of being happy?","o":["pleased","content","overjoyed","glad"],"a":2,"e":"**Overjoyed** means very, very happy. **Glad**, **pleased**, and **content** are milder."},
  {"id":"voc-169","skill":"v.relations","lv":4,"q":"Which word shows the STRONGEST feeling of being scared?","o":["worried","nervous","uneasy","terrified"],"a":3,"e":"**Terrified** means extremely scared. The other words show smaller amounts of fear."},
  {"id":"voc-170","skill":"v.relations","lv":4,"q":"Which word means the same as **begin**?","o":["finish","stop","start","wait"],"a":2,"e":"**Begin** and **start** are synonyms. Both mean to get something going."},
  {"id":"voc-171","skill":"v.relations","lv":4,"q":"Which word is an antonym for **ancient**?","o":["broken","old","famous","modern"],"a":3,"e":"**Ancient** means very old. Its opposite is **modern**, which means new or from today."},
  {"id":"voc-172","skill":"v.relations","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"The dog **ate** its dinner in about five seconds.\"\n\nWhich word would best show that the dog ate very quickly and hungrily?","o":["tasted","gobbled","nibbled","chewed"],"a":1,"e":"**Gobbled** means ate quickly and greedily. **Nibbled** means took tiny bites."},
  {"id":"voc-173","skill":"v.relations","lv":4,"q":"Which word shows the WEAKEST amount of cold?","o":["cool","icy","chilly","freezing"],"a":0,"e":"**Cool** is only a little cold. **Freezing** and **icy** are the coldest."},
  {"id":"voc-174","skill":"v.relations","lv":4,"q":"Which word is a synonym for **brave**?","o":["careful","gentle","courageous","frightened"],"a":2,"e":"**Courageous** and **brave** both mean ready to face danger or fear."},
  {"id":"voc-175","skill":"v.relations","lv":4,"q":"Which word is an antonym for **generous**?","o":["selfish","kind","giving","thankful"],"a":0,"e":"A **generous** person shares. A **selfish** person does not, so it is the opposite."},
  {"id":"voc-176","skill":"v.relations","lv":4,"q":"Which pair of words are synonyms?","o":["laugh and cry","whisper and shout","run and sit","shout and yell"],"a":3,"e":"**Shout** and **yell** both mean to speak very loudly."},
  {"id":"voc-177","skill":"v.relations","lv":4,"q":"Which pair of words are antonyms?","o":["smile and grin","float and sink","jump and leap","tidy and neat"],"a":1,"e":"**Float** means stay on top of water; **sink** means go to the bottom. They are opposites."},
  {"id":"voc-178","skill":"v.relations","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"Mia **walked** across the stage to get her award.\"\n\nWhich word would show that Mia walked proudly, with her head held high?","o":["shuffled","stumbled","strutted","tiptoed"],"a":2,"e":"**Strutted** means walked in a proud way. **Shuffled** and **stumbled** do not sound proud."},
  {"id":"voc-179","skill":"v.relations","lv":4,"q":"Which word shows the STRONGEST way to say someone is tired?","o":["exhausted","drowsy","sleepy","tired"],"a":0,"e":"**Exhausted** means extremely tired, with no energy left."},
  {"id":"voc-180","skill":"v.relations","lv":4,"q":"Which word means the same as **enormous**?","o":["little","gigantic","narrow","average"],"a":1,"e":"**Enormous** and **gigantic** both mean very, very big."},
  {"id":"voc-181","skill":"v.relations","lv":4,"q":"Which word is the opposite of **arrive**?","o":["visit","enter","reach","depart"],"a":3,"e":"To **arrive** is to get somewhere. To **depart** is to leave. They are opposites."},
  {"id":"voc-182","skill":"v.relations","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"The wind **blew** through the palm trees during the hurricane.\"\n\nWhich word would best show that the wind was very strong and loud?","o":["breezed","roared","drifted","whispered"],"a":1,"e":"**Roared** sounds loud and powerful, like a hurricane. **Whispered** sounds soft."},
  {"id":"voc-183","skill":"v.relations","lv":4,"q":"Which word is a synonym for **damp**?","o":["muddy","frozen","dry","moist"],"a":3,"e":"**Damp** and **moist** both mean a little wet."},
  {"id":"voc-184","skill":"v.relations","lv":4,"q":"Put these words in order from the smallest to the biggest amount of anger: **furious, annoyed, angry**.","o":["annoyed, angry, furious","furious, angry, annoyed","annoyed, furious, angry","angry, annoyed, furious"],"a":0,"e":"**Annoyed** is a little upset, **angry** is more, and **furious** is the most."},
  {"id":"voc-185","skill":"v.relations","lv":4,"q":"Which word is an antonym for **rough**?","o":["bumpy","hard","smooth","scratchy"],"a":2,"e":"**Rough** means bumpy or scratchy. The opposite is **smooth**."},
  {"id":"voc-186","skill":"v.relations","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"Ava **looked** at the tiny bug through the magnifying glass for a long time.\"\n\nWhich word best shows that Ava looked closely and carefully?","o":["blinked","peeked","examined","glanced"],"a":2,"e":"**Examined** means looked at closely. A **glance** or **peek** is very quick."},
  {"id":"voc-187","skill":"v.relations","lv":4,"q":"Which word is a synonym for **difficult**?","o":["simple","hard","easy","quick"],"a":1,"e":"**Difficult** and **hard** both describe something that takes a lot of effort."},
  {"id":"voc-188","skill":"v.relations","lv":4,"q":"Which word shows the STRONGEST feeling about food you like?","o":["adore","don't mind","like","enjoy"],"a":0,"e":"**Adore** means to love very much. It is stronger than **like** or **enjoy**."},
  {"id":"voc-189","skill":"v.relations","lv":4,"q":"Which word means the opposite of **victory**?","o":["win","trophy","game","defeat"],"a":3,"e":"A **victory** is a win. A **defeat** is a loss, so they are opposites."},
  {"id":"voc-190","skill":"v.relations","lv":4,"q":"Which word is the best synonym for **giggle** in \"The toddler began to giggle\"?","o":["cry","laugh","sneeze","hum"],"a":1,"e":"To **giggle** is to laugh in a light, silly way."},
  {"id":"voc-191","skill":"v.relations","lv":4,"q":"Which word is an antonym for **shallow**?","o":["wide","muddy","calm","deep"],"a":3,"e":"**Shallow** water is not deep. The opposite is **deep**."},
  {"id":"voc-192","skill":"v.relations","lv":4,"q":"Which **two** words are synonyms for **happy**? Choose **two** answers.","o":["sleepy","joyful","gloomy","grumpy","cheerful"],"a":[1,4],"e":"**Cheerful** and **joyful** both mean happy. **Gloomy** and **grumpy** are opposites."},
  {"id":"voc-193","skill":"v.relations","lv":4,"q":"Which **two** words are antonyms for **brave**? Choose **two** answers.","o":["heroic","bold","daring","cowardly","fearful"],"a":[3,4],"e":"**Cowardly** and **fearful** are opposites of brave. **Bold**, **daring**, and **heroic** mean almost the same as brave."},
  {"id":"voc-194","skill":"v.relations","lv":4,"q":"Which **two** words describe a sound that is very soft? Choose **two** answers.","o":["whisper","shriek","holler","bellow","murmur"],"a":[0,4],"e":"A **whisper** and a **murmur** are soft sounds. **Shriek**, **bellow**, and **holler** are loud."},
  {"id":"voc-195","skill":"v.relations","lv":4,"q":"Which **two** words describe moving very fast? Choose **two** answers.","o":["dash","stroll","crawl","wander","zoom"],"a":[0,4],"e":"To **dash** and to **zoom** both mean to move very fast."},
  {"id":"voc-196","skill":"v.relations","lv":5,"q":"Which word shows the STRONGEST feeling of dislike?","o":["mind","dislike","detest","disapprove"],"a":2,"e":"**Detest** means to hate very strongly. It is much stronger than **dislike** or **mind**."},
  {"id":"voc-197","skill":"v.relations","lv":5,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"The old map was **obsolete** because new roads had been built.\"\n\nWhich word is an antonym for **obsolete**?","o":["current","ancient","outdated","useless"],"a":0,"e":"**Obsolete** means out of date. **Current** means up to date, so it is the opposite."},
  {"id":"voc-198","skill":"v.relations","lv":5,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"Instead of saying the cake was 'good,' the judge wanted a word that showed it was truly excellent.\"\n\nWhich word should the judge choose?","o":["superb","decent","okay","fine"],"a":0,"e":"**Superb** means excellent. **Fine**, **decent**, and **okay** only mean good enough."},
  {"id":"voc-199","skill":"v.relations","lv":5,"q":"Which word means almost the same as **reluctant** but is a little stronger?","o":["eager","hesitant","curious","unwilling"],"a":3,"e":"**Reluctant** means not wanting to. **Unwilling** is stronger: it means refusing. **Hesitant** is about the same or weaker."},
  {"id":"voc-200","skill":"v.relations","lv":5,"q":"Which **two** words are synonyms for **abundant**? Choose **two** answers.","o":["ample","rare","plentiful","scant","scarce"],"a":[0,2],"e":"**Abundant** means more than enough. **Plentiful** and **ample** mean the same. **Scarce**, **rare**, and **scant** mean not enough."},
  {"id":"voc-201","skill":"v.academic","lv":4,"q":"Your teacher asks you to **compare** two stories. What should you do?","o":["tell only how the stories are different","draw a picture of a story","tell how the stories are alike","retell one story in order"],"a":2,"e":"To **compare** is to look at how things are alike. (Sometimes compare also includes differences.)"},
  {"id":"voc-202","skill":"v.academic","lv":4,"q":"Your teacher asks you to **contrast** a frog and a toad. What should you do?","o":["tell how they are the same","tell how they are different","list facts about frogs only","choose which one you like better"],"a":1,"e":"To **contrast** is to show how things are different."},
  {"id":"voc-203","skill":"v.academic","lv":4,"q":"What does it mean to **infer**?","o":["copy a sentence word for word, exactly as it is in the text","use clues and what you know to figure something out","make a wild guess without using any clues","read a story out loud to a partner"],"a":1,"e":"When you **infer**, you put text clues together with what you already know."},
  {"id":"voc-204","skill":"v.academic","lv":4,"q":"Which is the best example of **evidence** for the claim \"Manatees need warm water\"?","o":["Manatees are sometimes called sea cows.","Many people think manatees are the cutest animals in Florida.","Manatees are gray and can grow to be very large.","Manatees swim to warm springs when the ocean gets cold."],"a":3,"e":"**Evidence** is a fact that proves a point. Swimming to warm springs proves manatees need warmth."},
  {"id":"voc-205","skill":"v.academic","lv":4,"q":"What does **sequence** mean?","o":["the place and time where a story happens","a word that means the opposite","the order in which things happen","the main idea of a text"],"a":2,"e":"**Sequence** is the order of events: first, next, then, last."},
  {"id":"voc-206","skill":"v.academic","lv":4,"q":"Your teacher asks you to **summarize** a chapter. What should you do?","o":["tell the most important ideas in a few sentences","copy the first paragraph","give your opinion about the chapter","tell every single detail in the order it happened"],"a":0,"e":"A **summary** is short and gives only the most important ideas."},
  {"id":"voc-207","skill":"v.academic","lv":4,"q":"What does it mean to **analyze** a poem?","o":["look closely at its parts to understand it","read it very fast out loud to practice fluency","memorize the whole poem","write a new poem about the same topic"],"a":0,"e":"To **analyze** is to break something into parts and study them closely."},
  {"id":"voc-208","skill":"v.academic","lv":4,"q":"What is the **main idea** of a paragraph?","o":["the very last word in the paragraph","what the paragraph is mostly about","one small detail","the title of the book"],"a":1,"e":"The **main idea** is the big point, what the paragraph is mostly about."},
  {"id":"voc-209","skill":"v.academic","lv":4,"q":"Which sentence is an **opinion**?","o":["Ballet started in Europe hundreds of years ago.","Some dancers wear special shoes.","Dancing is the most fun hobby in the world.","Florida has many dance studios."],"a":2,"e":"An **opinion** is what someone thinks or feels. \"Most fun\" can't be proven, so it is an opinion."},
  {"id":"voc-210","skill":"v.academic","lv":4,"q":"What does it mean to **predict**?","o":["look up a word in the dictionary","list all the characters in the order they appear","explain what already happened","make a smart guess about what will happen next"],"a":3,"e":"To **predict** is to use clues to guess what will happen next."},
  {"id":"voc-211","skill":"v.academic","lv":4,"q":"In a story, the **conflict** is","o":["the problem the characters face","the person telling the story","the place where the story happens","the lesson of the story"],"a":0,"e":"The **conflict** is the problem. Stories are often about how characters solve it."},
  {"id":"voc-212","skill":"v.academic","lv":4,"q":"What does it mean to **cite** a source?","o":["make up your own facts","tell where your information came from","read a source silently","write the source's title in big letters"],"a":1,"e":"When you **cite** a source, you tell readers where you found the information, like \"According to the article...\""},
  {"id":"voc-213","skill":"v.academic","lv":4,"q":"Which word means \"to explain the meaning of something\"?","o":["interrupt","illustrate","imitate","interpret"],"a":3,"e":"To **interpret** is to explain what something means, like interpreting a poem or a chart."},
  {"id":"voc-214","skill":"v.academic","lv":4,"q":"What is a **claim** in an opinion essay?","o":["the last sentence","the title at the top of the essay","the writer's main opinion","a fact from a source"],"a":2,"e":"The **claim** is the main opinion that the whole essay tries to prove."},
  {"id":"voc-215","skill":"v.academic","lv":4,"q":"A **cause** is","o":["a word that describes","the end of a story","what happens as a result","why something happens"],"a":3,"e":"A **cause** is the reason something happens. The **effect** is what happens because of it."},
  {"id":"voc-216","skill":"v.academic","lv":4,"q":"Your teacher asks you to **describe** the setting. What should you do?","o":["tell what the place and time look and feel like","tell who the main character is","tell how the problem was solved","tell your favorite part of the story and why you liked it"],"a":0,"e":"To **describe** is to give details that help a reader picture something."},
  {"id":"voc-217","skill":"v.academic","lv":4,"q":"What does **relevant** mean when choosing details for an essay?","o":["from the end of the article","connected to the topic","funny or surprising","very long"],"a":1,"e":"A **relevant** detail is connected to your topic and helps prove your point."},
  {"id":"voc-218","skill":"v.academic","lv":4,"q":"What does it mean to **identify** the narrator?","o":["draw the narrator","tell why the story was written","tell who is telling the story","give the narrator a new name"],"a":2,"e":"To **identify** means to find and name something."},
  {"id":"voc-219","skill":"v.academic","lv":4,"q":"Which **two** words are about finding how things are alike or different? Choose **two** answers.","o":["summarize","compare","cite","predict","contrast"],"a":[1,4],"e":"When you **compare** and **contrast**, you look at how things are alike and different."},
  {"id":"voc-220","skill":"v.academic","lv":4,"q":"Which **two** actions are part of writing a good **conclusion**? Choose **two** answers.","o":["list every fact again","start with \"Once upon a time\"","leave the reader with a final thought","add a brand-new topic","restate your main idea"],"a":[2,4],"e":"A strong **conclusion** sums up your main idea and ends with a final thought for the reader."},
  {"id":"voc-221","skill":"r.figurative","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"Zoe spun across the stage like a top, twirling faster and faster.\"\n\nWhat does the simile show about Zoe?","o":["She was afraid to dance.","She was wearing a colorful, sparkly costume.","She was feeling dizzy and sick.","She was spinning quickly and smoothly."],"a":3,"e":"A top spins fast and smoothly. Comparing Zoe to a top shows how quickly she twirled."},
  {"id":"voc-222","skill":"r.figurative","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"My backpack weighed a ton after I packed all my library books.\"\n\nWhat does the phrase \"weighed a ton\" mean?","o":["The backpack was too small.","The backpack weighed 2,000 pounds.","The backpack was very heavy.","The backpack was broken."],"a":2,"e":"This is hyperbole, a big exaggeration. The backpack did not really weigh a ton; it just felt very heavy."},
  {"id":"voc-223","skill":"r.figurative","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"The old wooden floor groaned as we tiptoed across the attic.\"\n\nWhat kind of figurative language is used in this sentence?","o":["simile","personification","idiom","hyperbole"],"a":1,"e":"Floors can't really groan like a person. Giving a human action to a thing is **personification**."},
  {"id":"voc-224","skill":"r.figurative","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"When Liam saw his surprise party, he was all smiles.\"\n\nWhat does the phrase \"all smiles\" mean?","o":["very happy","covered in frosting","quiet and shy","confused"],"a":0,"e":"Being \"all smiles\" is an idiom that means looking very happy."},
  {"id":"voc-225","skill":"r.figurative","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"The classroom was a zoo after the fire drill.\"\n\nWhat does the metaphor tell you about the classroom?","o":["It was empty.","It had lots of animals in it.","It was loud and wild.","It was very clean."],"a":2,"e":"A zoo is full of noisy animals. Saying the class \"was a zoo\" shows it was loud and out of control."},
  {"id":"voc-226","skill":"r.figurative","lv":4,"q":"Which sentence uses a simile?","o":["The manatee was as gentle as a sleeping baby.","The manatee is a huge water mammal.","The manatee swam slowly through the clear, cool spring.","The manatee ate a pile of sea grass."],"a":0,"e":"A simile compares two things using \"like\" or \"as.\" The manatee is compared to a sleeping baby with \"as.\""},
  {"id":"voc-227","skill":"r.figurative","lv":4,"q":"Which sentence uses personification?","o":["The sun set slowly behind the tall trees.","The sun was very hot at noon.","The sun is a star.","The sun smiled down on the beach."],"a":3,"e":"The sun can't really smile. Giving it a human action makes it **personification**."},
  {"id":"voc-228","skill":"r.figurative","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"'Break a leg!' Mom said as I walked onto the stage for my dance recital.\"\n\nWhat does Mom mean?","o":["Be careful not to fall.","Good luck!","Hurry up!","Dance as fast as you can."],"a":1,"e":"\"Break a leg\" is an idiom performers use to wish each other good luck."},
  {"id":"voc-229","skill":"r.figurative","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"Our puppy, Biscuit, is a little tornado when he plays in the backyard.\"\n\nWhat does the metaphor show about Biscuit?","o":["He runs around wildly with lots of energy.","He is afraid of storms and hides under the bed.","He is very small and quiet.","He likes to dig holes all over the yard."],"a":0,"e":"A tornado spins and messes everything up. Calling Biscuit a tornado shows how wild and energetic he is."},
  {"id":"voc-230","skill":"r.figurative","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"I'm so hungry I could eat a horse!\"\n\nWhat kind of figurative language is this?","o":["metaphor","hyperbole","simile","personification"],"a":1,"e":"No one could really eat a horse! This huge exaggeration is **hyperbole**. It shows the speaker is very hungry."},
  {"id":"voc-231","skill":"r.figurative","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"The squishy bunny toy felt as soft as a cloud.\"\n\nWhat does the simile help the reader understand?","o":["how big the toy was","which store the toy came from","what color the toy was","how very soft the toy was"],"a":3,"e":"Clouds look very soft. Comparing the toy to a cloud helps you imagine how soft it felt."},
  {"id":"voc-232","skill":"r.figurative","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"When the teacher announced a pop quiz, my heart sank.\"\n\nWhat does \"my heart sank\" mean?","o":["I needed to sit down and catch my breath.","I felt very excited.","I suddenly felt disappointed or worried.","My heart stopped beating."],"a":2,"e":"\"My heart sank\" is an idiom for a sudden sad or worried feeling."},
  {"id":"voc-233","skill":"r.figurative","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"The wind howled through the trees all night during the storm.\"\n\nWhy does the author say the wind \"howled\"?","o":["to show the storm was over and the sky was clearing","to show a wolf was in the forest","to show the wind was soft and gentle, like a sigh","to show the wind was loud and wild, like an animal"],"a":3,"e":"Howling is a sound animals make. Saying the wind howled helps you hear how loud and fierce it was."},
  {"id":"voc-234","skill":"r.figurative","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"Grandpa said finishing the puzzle would be a piece of cake.\"\n\nWhat does \"a piece of cake\" mean?","o":["very easy","very tasty","a reward","very messy"],"a":0,"e":"\"A piece of cake\" is an idiom that means something is easy to do."},
  {"id":"voc-235","skill":"r.figurative","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"The stars were diamonds scattered across the night sky.\"\n\nWhat two things are being compared in this metaphor?","o":["diamonds and the night","stars and the sky","stars and diamonds","the sky and the night"],"a":2,"e":"The metaphor says the stars WERE diamonds. It compares stars to sparkling jewels."},
  {"id":"voc-236","skill":"r.figurative","lv":4,"q":"Which sentence uses hyperbole?","o":["Your room is messier than mine.","I have told you a million times to clean your room!","I have told you three times today to clean your room.","Please clean your room before dinner."],"a":1,"e":"Saying \"a million times\" is a big exaggeration. That is **hyperbole**."},
  {"id":"voc-237","skill":"r.figurative","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"Ella was as busy as a bee getting ready for the bake sale.\"\n\nWhat does the simile mean?","o":["Ella was buzzing around like an insect.","Ella was making jars of honey to sell at the bake sale.","Ella was working hard and moving from task to task.","Ella was afraid of getting stung."],"a":2,"e":"Bees are always working. \"Busy as a bee\" means very busy and hardworking."},
  {"id":"voc-238","skill":"r.figurative","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"Our dance teacher said, 'Let's not cry over spilled milk. We'll practice harder next time.'\"\n\nWhat does \"cry over spilled milk\" mean?","o":["feel sad about wasting good food that someone could eat","be upset about something that is done and can't be changed","be afraid of making mistakes in front of the whole class","clean up any spill as fast as you can so nobody slips and falls"],"a":1,"e":"This idiom means don't stay upset about something that is already done. The teacher wants the dancers to move forward."},
  {"id":"voc-239","skill":"r.figurative","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"The leaves danced in the autumn breeze.\"\n\nWhat does this personification help the reader picture?","o":["leaves falling straight down and landing in a heavy pile","people dancing at a party","trees growing new leaves","leaves moving lightly and gracefully in the wind"],"a":3,"e":"Leaves can't really dance, but saying they did helps you picture them moving lightly and gracefully."},
  {"id":"voc-240","skill":"r.figurative","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"Jake is a night owl; he loves to read until midnight.\"\n\nWhat does \"night owl\" mean?","o":["a person who likes to stay up late","a bird that hunts at night","a person who is very wise","a person who is afraid of the dark woods"],"a":0,"e":"Owls are awake at night. Calling Jake a \"night owl\" means he likes to stay up late."},
  {"id":"voc-241","skill":"r.figurative","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"The thunder was a giant drum booming across the sky.\"\n\nWhat does this metaphor help the reader understand?","o":["how loud and deep the thunder sounded","that the storm was far away","that someone was playing music outside","what color the sky was"],"a":0,"e":"A giant drum would be very loud and booming. The metaphor helps you hear the thunder."},
  {"id":"voc-242","skill":"r.figurative","lv":4,"q":"Which sentence uses an idiom?","o":["I studied for the math test for an hour after dinner.","The math test was so hard, it was over my head.","My math test is in my folder.","The math test had twenty questions."],"a":1,"e":"\"Over my head\" is an idiom. It means too hard to understand."},
  {"id":"voc-243","skill":"r.figurative","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"The alarm clock screamed at 6:00 a.m.\"\n\nWhat does the word \"screamed\" show about the alarm clock?","o":["It was quiet, soft, and gentle.","It was broken.","It was very loud and annoying.","It was scared."],"a":2,"e":"Clocks can't really scream. This personification shows the alarm was loud and hard to ignore."},
  {"id":"voc-244","skill":"r.figurative","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"After practice, my legs felt like jelly.\"\n\nWhat does the simile mean?","o":["My legs were covered in sticky jam.","My legs felt sticky.","My legs felt strong.","My legs felt weak and wobbly."],"a":3,"e":"Jelly is wobbly. Saying your legs feel like jelly means they feel weak and shaky."},
  {"id":"voc-245","skill":"r.figurative","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"Maria's smile was sunshine on a gray day.\"\n\nWhat does the metaphor show about Maria's smile?","o":["It only happened on days when it rained.","It made people feel happy and warm.","It was very bright yellow.","It made people squint."],"a":1,"e":"Sunshine on a gray day cheers people up. The metaphor shows Maria's smile brightened everyone's mood."},
  {"id":"voc-246","skill":"r.figurative","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"'Hold your horses!' said Coach Rivera when we started running before the whistle.\"\n\nWhat does Coach Rivera mean?","o":["Hold on tight.","Ride faster.","Take care of the animals.","Wait! Don't go yet."],"a":3,"e":"\"Hold your horses\" is an idiom that means slow down and wait."},
  {"id":"voc-247","skill":"r.figurative","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"The waves whispered secrets to the sand.\"\n\nWhich word in the sentence shows personification?","o":["sand","secrets","whispered","waves"],"a":2,"e":"Waves can't whisper; people do. The word \"whispered\" gives a human action to the waves."},
  {"id":"voc-248","skill":"r.figurative","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"This backpack is older than the dinosaurs!\"\n\nWhat is the speaker really saying?","o":["The backpack is very old.","The backpack is from a museum.","The backpack was found with fossils.","The backpack has a dinosaur on it."],"a":0,"e":"That's hyperbole! The backpack isn't millions of years old. The speaker just means it is very old."},
  {"id":"voc-249","skill":"r.figurative","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"Toby felt like a fish out of water at his new school.\"\n\nWhat does \"a fish out of water\" mean?","o":["someone who is a really great swimmer","someone who is very happy","someone who feels out of place","someone who is very thirsty"],"a":2,"e":"A fish doesn't belong on land. The idiom means someone who feels uncomfortable in a new place."},
  {"id":"voc-250","skill":"r.figurative","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"The hurricane roared toward the coast, swallowing everything in its path.\"\n\nWhat does the phrase \"swallowing everything in its path\" help show?","o":["that the storm was small and would pass quickly","that the storm was hungry like an animal","that the storm was moving away","how powerful and destructive the storm was"],"a":3,"e":"Storms can't swallow, but the personification shows how much damage the hurricane could do."},
  {"id":"voc-251","skill":"r.figurative","lv":4,"q":"Which sentence uses a metaphor?","o":["My little brother is three years old.","My little brother is a chatterbox.","My little brother talks a lot.","My little brother talks like a parrot."],"a":1,"e":"A metaphor says one thing IS another, without \"like\" or \"as.\" The brother is called a chatterbox."},
  {"id":"voc-252","skill":"r.figurative","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"When she saw the giant pile of homework, Lucy said, 'This will take me a hundred years!'\"\n\nWhat does Lucy's hyperbole show about her feelings?","o":["She thinks the homework will take a very long time.","She has already finished all of the homework by herself.","She plans to finish the homework next century.","She is excited about the homework."],"a":0,"e":"Lucy exaggerates to show the homework looks like it will take forever."},
  {"id":"voc-253","skill":"r.figurative","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"Ms. Patel said the field trip was 'in the bag.'\"\n\nWhat does \"in the bag\" mean?","o":["packed in a backpack","a secret","canceled","certain to happen"],"a":3,"e":"When something is \"in the bag,\" it is sure to happen or succeed."},
  {"id":"voc-254","skill":"r.figurative","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"The cat's eyes were glowing lanterns in the dark garage.\"\n\nWhat does this metaphor help the reader picture?","o":["the garage lights turning on","a cat holding a light","the cat's eyes shining brightly in the dark","a lantern sitting on a shelf next to the cat"],"a":2,"e":"Lanterns glow in the dark. The metaphor helps you picture the cat's eyes shining."},
  {"id":"voc-255","skill":"r.figurative","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"The crowd at the parade was a sea of green shirts.\"\n\nWhat does \"a sea of green shirts\" mean?","o":["a huge crowd of people wearing green","people swimming in green water","green shirts floating out in the ocean waves","a few people in green shirts"],"a":0,"e":"A sea is huge. The metaphor shows there were so many people in green shirts that they looked like a sea."},
  {"id":"voc-256","skill":"r.figurative","lv":4,"q":"Which **two** sentences use similes? Choose **two** answers.","o":["The leaves whispered in the breeze.","My bedroom is a disaster area.","Sami ran like the wind to catch the bus.","The gator's back was as bumpy as a road full of potholes.","The cookies were burned to a crisp."],"a":[2,3],"e":"Similes use \"like\" or \"as\" to compare. The gator's back is compared \"as bumpy as\" a road, and Sami ran \"like\" the wind."},
  {"id":"voc-257","skill":"r.figurative","lv":4,"q":"Which **two** sentences use personification? Choose **two** answers.","o":["The old car coughed and sputtered up the hill.","The flowers were as red as strawberries.","The car was a rocket on the highway.","The flowers nodded their heads in the breeze.","I have waited forever for this car ride."],"a":[0,3],"e":"Flowers can't nod and cars can't cough. Those sentences give human actions to things."},
  {"id":"voc-258","skill":"r.figurative","lv":4,"q":"Which **two** sentences use hyperbole? Choose **two** answers.","o":["My dad snores every night.","I've been waiting here for a thousand years.","The train was on time today.","My dad snores louder than a freight train.","I waited ten minutes for the bus."],"a":[1,3],"e":"Snoring louder than a train and waiting a thousand years are huge exaggerations. That is hyperbole."},
  {"id":"voc-259","skill":"r.figurative","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"Mateo was on cloud nine after he scored the winning goal, and he was grinning from ear to ear.\"\n\nWhich **two** phrases are idioms that show Mateo was very happy? Choose **two** answers.","o":["grinning from ear to ear","Mateo was","after he scored","scored the winning goal","on cloud nine"],"a":[0,4],"e":"\"On cloud nine\" and \"grinning from ear to ear\" are idioms that mean extremely happy."},
  {"id":"voc-260","skill":"r.figurative","lv":4,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"The tiny hummingbird was a jewel buzzing from flower to flower; its wings moved as fast as a blink.\"\n\nWhich **two** types of figurative language are in this sentence? Choose **two** answers.","o":["simile","idiom","metaphor","hyperbole","personification"],"a":[0,2],"e":"\"The hummingbird was a jewel\" is a metaphor. \"As fast as a blink\" uses \"as,\" so it is a simile."},
  {"id":"voc-261","skill":"r.figurative","lv":5,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"The library was a treasure chest waiting to be opened.\"\n\nWhat does the metaphor suggest about the library?","o":["It is very old, dusty, and hard to find.","It is full of valuable things to discover.","It holds gold coins and jewels.","It is locked and very hard for people to enter."],"a":1,"e":"A treasure chest holds valuable things. The metaphor shows the library is full of wonderful books to discover."},
  {"id":"voc-262","skill":"r.figurative","lv":5,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"After losing the championship, the team's spirits sank like a stone.\"\n\nWhat does the simile show about how the team felt?","o":["They felt very discouraged very quickly.","They felt heavy and tired from playing.","They fell into a lake.","They slowly started to feel better after the game."],"a":0,"e":"A stone sinks fast and all the way to the bottom. The simile shows the team's hope dropped fast."},
  {"id":"voc-263","skill":"r.figurative","lv":5,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"Ruby had butterflies in her stomach before her first solo dance.\"\n\nWhat does the idiom mean, and why does the author use it?","o":["Ruby was excited about bugs; it shows she likes nature.","Ruby was hungry; it shows she skipped lunch and wanted a snack.","Ruby was sick; it shows she ate something bad.","Ruby felt nervous; it shows how jittery the feeling was."],"a":3,"e":"\"Butterflies in your stomach\" means feeling nervous. Butterflies flutter, just like a nervous tummy feels."},
  {"id":"voc-264","skill":"r.figurative","lv":5,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"Time crawled during the long car ride to Key West.\"\n\nWhat does the personification in this sentence suggest?","o":["The ride was over very quickly.","The ride felt very long and slow.","The car was moving on its hands and knees.","The driver was lost."],"a":1,"e":"Crawling is slow. Saying time \"crawled\" shows the ride seemed to last forever."},
  {"id":"voc-265","skill":"r.figurative","lv":5,"q":"Read the lines from a poem.\n\n*The moon is a silver coin\ntossed high into the night,\nspinning slowly over the sea,\nand dropping out of sight.*\n\nWhat does the poet compare the moon to, and what does it suggest?","o":["the sea, suggesting the moon is wet, blue, and full of waves","a bird, suggesting the moon has wings and can fly and sing in the night sky","a coin, suggesting the moon is round, shiny, and moving across the sky","the night, suggesting the moon is dark and scary"],"a":2,"e":"The metaphor compares the moon to a silver coin. Coins are round and shiny, and tossing one shows the moon moving across the sky."},
  {"id":"voc-266","skill":"r.figurative","lv":5,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"When Dad saw the broken vase, he hit the roof.\"\n\nWhat does \"hit the roof\" mean?","o":["became very angry","climbed onto the house","jumped with joy","fixed the ceiling"],"a":0,"e":"\"Hit the roof\" is an idiom that means became very angry very suddenly."},
  {"id":"voc-267","skill":"r.figurative","lv":5,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"The old oak stood guard over the playground, stretching its arms to shade the children.\"\n\nHow does the personification add to the meaning?","o":["It shows the playground is empty.","It shows the tree is about to fall.","It makes the tree seem scary, mean, and dangerous.","It makes the tree seem caring and protective."],"a":3,"e":"\"Stood guard\" and \"stretching its arms to shade\" make the tree seem like a kind protector."},
  {"id":"voc-268","skill":"r.figurative","lv":5,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"The test was a mountain, and Sofia was determined to climb it one step at a time.\"\n\nWhat does the metaphor show about how Sofia feels about the test?","o":["She would rather go hiking up in the tall mountains than take a hard test.","It seems big and hard, but she plans to work through it bit by bit.","She thinks the test is unfair.","It is so easy that she will finish it before anyone else."],"a":1,"e":"A mountain is big and tough to climb. Climbing \"one step at a time\" shows Sofia will keep working steadily."},
  {"id":"voc-269","skill":"r.figurative","lv":5,"q":"Read the sentence.\n\n\"'Let's get the ball rolling,' said the club president, 'so we can finish planning the fundraiser.'\"\n\nWhat does \"get the ball rolling\" mean?","o":["take a break","clean up","get started","play a game"],"a":2,"e":"\"Get the ball rolling\" is an idiom that means to start something."},
  {"id":"voc-270","skill":"r.figurative","lv":5,"q":"Which **two** sentences use figurative language to show that a character is calm? Choose **two** answers.","o":["Kai was boiling mad after the game.","Kai's heart pounded like a drum.","Grandma's voice was a gentle breeze.","Grandma's voice was a fire alarm.","Kai stayed as cool as a cucumber during the spelling bee."],"a":[2,4],"e":"A gentle breeze and \"cool as a cucumber\" both suggest calm. A fire alarm and a pounding heart suggest the opposite."}
];
